Some pulsars display highly irregular pulses, classified as Rotating Radio Transients (RRATs) \citep{rrats2006}. The origin of this sporadic emission remains uncertain, with several hypotheses proposed but yet to be confirmed. \citet{0656rrat} suggest that RRATs may simply be normal pulsars with exceptionally bright emission features, where large distances could render their average signal too faint to form a stable integrated pulse profile, while sporadic bright pulses remain detectable. Alternatively, \citet{burkeandbailes2010} propose that RRATs may represent a late evolutionary stage of pulsars. Despite these hypotheses, the precise nature of RRATs remains an open question, and they are currently classified as a distinct subclass of pulsars characterized by extreme intermittency in their emission. Due to this behavior of RRATs, it is difficult to form an average profile for these pulsars as we need several hundreds or even 1000s of pulses. Consequently, RRATs are not considered an ideal source for timing observations. In this paper, we will concentrate on a unique case of PSR~B0656+14 which behaves like an RRAT but closer to us compared to the other such sources.

