\begin{abstract}
$\fc$ is a first-order logic that reasons over all factors of a finite word using concatenation, and can define non-regular languages like that of all squares ($ww$).
In this paper, we establish that there are regular languages that are not $\fc$-definable.
Moreover, we give a decidable characterization of the $\fc$-definable regular languages in terms of algebra, automata, and regular expressions.
The latter of which is natural and concise: Star-free generalized regular expressions extended with the Kleene star of terminal words.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Finite model theory, first-order logic, regular languages.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

