Figure~\ref{fig:conceptual_conversation} provides a conceptual illustration of (b) the preference elicitation stage and (c) the advisory discussion during the stock assessment stage.
The user study is organized into a series of tasks designed to evaluate two financial advisory systems—1st System and 2nd System. Each system consists of a different financial advisor and includes four main stages. The process begins with the a) System Description, where participants are given a general overview of the financial advisory. Also, participants are given an \emphasize{investor narrative}: a description of a hypothetical investor that they are to role-play throughout the system in this stage.

In the (b) User Preference Elicitation Stage, an advisor aims to gather relevant information from participants through interactive, multi-turn conversations, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:conceptual_conversation} (b). The advisor proactively inquires about the user's preferences and clarifies any unfamiliar concepts. During this stage, the advisor collects a predefined set of investment preferences corresponding to each investor narrative. The conversation continues until the advisor determines that sufficient information has been obtained. Once the conversation ends, the advisor will generate a user profile that describes the participants’ investment preferences regarding the predefined set of items.

Following the User Preference Elicitation Stage, the (c) Stock Assessment Stage involves an interactive task where participants evaluate a candidate stock in each round. This stage includes advisory discussions where participants decide whether a candidate stock suits their investment profile by conversing with the advisor, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:conceptual_conversation} (c). 
For each stock, we provide data relevant to making financial decisions as detailed in Section~\ref{sec:dataset}. The advisor uses this data to answer participants' questions. If a query cannot be answered with the advisor’s internal knowledge or the provided data, the advisor is prompted to state that they cannot answer the question to mitigate hallucinations.  After an advisory discussion in each round, participants indicate on a 7-point Likert scale how likely they are to purchase the stock and provide their reasons in a free text format in evaluation.
The system session concludes with the d) System Evaluation, where participants assess the relevance of four stocks to their investor profiles in ranking and provide feedback on various dimensions of the system's performance. The question about each dimension is answered on a seven-point Likert scale. 
Simply asking participants explicit questions like \emphasize{How much did you like the system?} is considered inadequate and suffers from a lack of content validity~\cite{ricci2010introduction}; such questions can lead to varied interpretations and unreliable data due to the subjective nature of the term \emphasize{like.} To enhance the validity of our experiments, we ask about each response dimension with operational definitions, as detailed in Table~\ref{tab:operational_definition}. This approach ensures that all participants have a consistent understanding of each dimension. Both the response dimensions and the operations definitions are inspired the past studies as illustrated in Table~\ref{tab:operational_definition}.

