\subsubsection{Conclusion:} Overall, the findings on decision-making effectiveness suggest that successful elicitation is essential for effective personalization. When preference elicitation is successful, the personalized advisor significantly improves the quality of financial decisions. In contrast, if elicitation fails, the advisor may direct investors toward unsuitable assets.
On the other hand, even when the personalized advisor succeeds in user elicitation (elicitation accuracy $\geq$ 50\%), Spearman’s rho remains $0.504$, and it is still far from the ground truth. This highlights the need to further improve the advisor’s design to enhance decisions as well as improve the user's assessment of the advisor.
Finally, the results also indicate that, although personalizing the investment stance can enhance decision-making accuracy, it does not necessarily have a significant impact on how users assess financial advisors. For example, no significant difference is observed in the \emphasize{Perceived Personalization} dimension between the two advisors. Research on perceived personalization indicates that users’ perception of personalized messages depends more on how well the received content aligns with their expectations than on the underlying personalization process~\cite{li2016does}. Likewise, users may be insensitive to differences in advice quality when forming their subjective impressions of the system.
