\section{Asset Selection and Scoring}\label{app:assets}

In our study, we employed a rule-based approach to calculate the relevance scores of companies listed in the S\&P 500 index\footnote{The S\&P 500 is a stock market index comprising 503 common stocks issued by large-cap companies traded on American stock exchanges.}.  This scoring ranged from 0 to 3, based on how many of the investor's key investment preferences in each investor narrative a company met. 
% 
In particular, for the industry preference, we assessed whether the industry classification of each company matched the preferred industry stated in the investor narratives\footnote{The sector data is taken from \href{https://finance.yahoo.com/}{Yahoo! Finance}.}.
%%
Next, regarding stock style, we distinguished between growth and value stocks, which are among the most widely used investment styles~\cite{fama1998value}.
A value stock is typically considered undervalued based on key financial metrics, whereas a growth stock is expected to grow faster than the market average.
A company listed in the \href{https://www.spglobal.com/spdji/en/indices/equity/sp-500-value/#overview}{S\&P500 Value} was categorized as a value stock, while those in the \href{https://www.spglobal.com/spdji/en/indices/equity/sp-500-growth/#overview}{S\&P500 Growth} were considered growth stocks\footnote{Value stocks are categorized based on ratios of book value, earnings, and sales to price, while growth stocks are categorized based on sales growth, the ratio of earnings change to price, and momentum.}.
% 
Additionally, we evaluated dividend payment preferences by identifying companies known as dividend aristocrats, which are recognized for their consistent, long-term dividend growth\footnote{\url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S\%26P_500_Dividend_Aristocrats}}.
% 
For sensitivity to global market changes, we consulted the Morningstar stock sector structure\footnote{\url{https://www.morningstar.com/content/dam/marketing/apac/au/pdfs/Legal/StockSectorStructure_Factsheet.pdf}} to differentiate between cyclical and non-cyclical stocks. Cyclical stocks are those that are heavily influenced by macroeconomic changes and tend to perform well when the economy is growing, whereas non-cyclical stocks, also known as defensive stocks, provide consistent performance regardless of economic conditions. 


After calculating the relevance scores for companies listed on the S\&P 500 for each investor narrative, we selected four companies with varying relevance scores (from 0 to 3) to match the stocks with the investor.  To ensure that participants will not encounter the same stock more than once during the experiment, we avoided any overlap among the companies selected. Additionally, we consulted a financial expert to verify whether the relevance scores were reasonable. 
