It remains to specify the exogenous i.i.d.~shocks $\epsilon_k$ in the calibrated  dynamics:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:non-gaussian OU}
    X^{\ell,d}_{k+1} = X^{\ell,d}_{k} + \alpha^{\ell}_{R(F^{\ell,d}_k)} (F^{\ell,d}_k- X^{\ell,d}_{k})  + \sigma^{\ell}_{R(F^{\ell,d}_k)} \cdot \epsilon^{\ell}_{k} \qquad\text{ for } k = 0,1,\ldots,23,
\end{equation}
After calibrating $\alpha^\ell_{\cdot}, \sigma^\ell_{\cdot} $, the residuals,  
$\mathcal{E}^{l}_\cdot$, across bins 
reveal that the shocks $\epsilon^\ell_k$ deviate from a Gaussian distribution, cf.~supplementary Figure \ref{fig:residuals} in Appendix \ref{Appen_calibrate}. In addition, we need to simultaneously constrain $X^{\ell,d}_{k+1} \in [0,1]$ and allow for the positive probability of hitting the boundaries $[0,1]$ which occurs frequently, especially when the forecast similarly satisfies $F^{\ell,d}_k \in \{0,1\}$, see the actual trajectory of the generation ratio $(A^{\ell,d}_k)$ in the right panel of Figure \ref{fig:Binned Calibration}. The latter is another reason why we do not use the Jacobi SDE \eqref{eq:OU} whose trajectories stay in the interior $(0,1)$ a.s.
To address these issues we make two adjustments for the distribution of $(\epsilon^\ell_k)$. 
First, we replace the Gaussian assumption with a bootstrapping approach. 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:non-gaussianOU_boostrap}
    X^{\ell,d}_{k+1} = X^{\ell,d}_{k} + \alpha^{\ell}_{R(F^{\ell,d}_k)} (F^{\ell,d}_k- X^{\ell,d}_{k})  + \sigma^{\ell}_{R(F^{\ell,d}_k)} \cdot \epsilon^{\ell}_{R(F^{\ell,d}_k)} \qquad\text{ for } k = 0,1,\ldots,23,
\end{equation}
where the shocks $ \epsilon^{\ell}_{r}$ are generated by bootstrapping samples from the binned distribution of residuals \( \mathcal{E}^{\ell}_{r} \). Second, to maintain the trajectories of $(X^{\ell,d}_k)$ within $[0,1]$ and reach the boundaries with positive probability, we further modify our shocks $\epsilon^{\ell}_{r}$. In particular, consider the case when \( R(F^{\ell,d}_k) = 1 \), corresponding to the first decile bin. Since all forecasts within this bin are equal to zero, we have \( F^{\ell,d}_k = 0 \). We generate $\epsilon^{\ell}_{1}$ by using a  mixed distribution, adding a point mass at zero for $\epsilon^{\ell}_{1}$. 
To do so, we first sample $Z^{\ell}_{1} \sim 1-\Bern(p^{\ell}_{1})$ and then set
\begin{align}\label{eq:bootstrap}
\epsilon^{\ell}_{1}= \begin{cases}0, & \text {if } Z^{\ell}_{1}=0; \\ \epsilon^{\ell,+}_{1}, & \text {if } Z^{\ell}_{1}=1,\end{cases}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon^{\ell,+}_{1}$ is a positive sample from $\mathcal{E}^{\ell}_{1}$. A natural estimate for the hyperparameter $p^\ell_1$ is $P(X^{\ell,d}_{k+1}=0| X^{\ell,d}_{k}=0)$. Since $X^{\ell,d}_{k}$ is not directly available, we rely on $F^{\ell,d}_{k}$ which gives rise to: 
\begin{equation*}
    \hat{p}^{\ell}_{1} = \frac{\sum_{(d,k) \in \mathcal{N}^{\ell}_{1}} \mathbb{1}_{\{F^{\ell,d}_k=0, F^{\ell,d}_{k+1}=0\}}}{\sum_{(d,k) \in \mathcal{N}^{\ell}_{1}} \mathbb{1}_{\{F^{\ell,d}_k=0\}}}.
\end{equation*}
This ensures that the simulated wind generation ratios $X^{\ell,d}_k$ are non-negative and not positively biased when $F^{\ell,d}_k=0$. The resulting probability of zero generation $\hat{p}^{\ell}_{1}$ ranges from $72\%$ to $78\%$ across $149$ Texas-7k wind units. Therefore, with high probability, our trajectories hit the boundaries when $F_k = 0$. We apply similar modification of our shocks $ \epsilon^{\ell}_{10}$ where $F^{\ell,d}_k=1$. In this case, the probability of maximum generation $\hat{p}^\ell_{10}$ ranges from $24\%$ to $47\%$. See \citeA{li2024probabilisticspatiotemporalmodelingdayahead} for related multi-site calibration. 


The right panel of Figure \ref{fig:Binned Calibration} shows the simulated generation ratio trajectories of a representative wind asset, Foard City Wind Farm, on 2018-04-05. The Figure shows the forecast generation ratio $(F^{\ell,d}_k)$, the actual generation ratio $(A^{\ell,d}_k)$ and five simulated trajectories $(X^{\ell,d}_k)$. The Figure also demonstrates the $80\%$ scenario band, obtained via quantiles of $10,000$ Monte Carlo trajectories  $\bold{X}^{\ell,d}_\cdot$ generated according to \eqref{eq:non-gaussianOU_boostrap}. Notice the narrowing band between hours 15-19 when the forecast is zero, illustrating \eqref{eq:bootstrap}. The empirical time-averaged coverage rate $\mathrm{ECR}^{\ell,d}$ in this case is $83\%$, obtained  via 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ecr}
    \mathrm{ECR}^{\ell,d} := \frac{1}{24} \sum_{k=0}^{23} \mathbb{1} ( A^{\ell,d}_k\geq q_{0.1} (\bold{X}^{\ell,d}_k)\text{ and }   A^{\ell,d}_k \leq q_{0.9} (\bold{X}^{\ell,d}_{k})),
\end{equation}
where  $q_\alpha(\cdot)$ denotes the $\alpha$-th quantile. The averaged empirical coverage rate $\overline{\mathrm{ECR}}^{\ell}=\frac{1}{365} \sum_{d=1}^{365} \mathrm{ECR}^{\ell,d}$ ranges from 74\% to 88\% across the assets, see Appendix \ref{Appen_calibrate} Figure \ref{fig:80CIsites}. Given that the theoretical nominal coverage is 80\%, this suggests that the calibrated discretized SDE model performs reasonably well but exhibits some site-to-site variability. 

\subsection{SHADOw-GP Evaluation}\label{sec:5.2}

To assess the impact of hybridization, we consider retrofitting the wind assets in Texas-7k with a three-hour BESS, $\Imax = 3 B_{\max}$ with capacity \( B_{\text{max}} = 0.10 \) representing \( 10\% \) of the nameplate generation capacity (recall that in this section $X_k$ is rescaled to $[0,1]$). Our \( B_{\max} \) is slightly higher than $1$ standard deviation of the hourly forecast errors, $\{ A^{\ell,d}_k - F^{\ell,d}_k \}_{(d,k)\in N_{l}}$, which ranges between \( 0.05 \) and \( 0.08 \). 
The charging efficiency parameter is set to \( \eta = 0.95 \) for 10\% round-trip losses, while \( \text{SoC}_{\min} = 0.05 \) and \( \text{SoC}_{\max} = 0.95 \), which means that the BESS has a \( 5\% \) SoC buffer. \citeA{LBNL} reports that existing wind-hybrid BESS roundtrip efficiencies, computed as the ratio of aggregate discharged energy relative to aggregate charged energy, in US are in the range $75-95\%$. We use the same simulation design as the case study in Section \ref{ssec:4.5}. We use hourly steps with $K=24$ total steps. We utilize the same running cost $\tilde{f}$ and terminal cost $g$ as in the stationary example for all our experiments, with terminal penalty $\mathcal{P}=1$, which was re-scaled to the numerical scale of $\Imax$. The rest of the section utilizes the same case study as in the previous section: Foard City Wind Farm on 2018-04-05.

The pervasive time-dependence of $(X_k)$ makes the solution, i.e.~the maps $\hat{Q}_k, \hat{B}_k$ also strongly dependent on $k$.
The SHADOw-GP algorithm automatically adapts the training domain $(\mathcal{D}^v_k)$ at each step $k$ to reflect the time-varying distribution of $(X_k)$, see the $x$-axes in Figure \ref{fig:Training design} that shows $\bar{\mathcal{D}}^v_k$ at two different hours. Figure \ref{fig:Training design} also illustrates the (kernel-smoothed) density of $10,000$ Monte Carlo trajectories of optimally dispatched $(X_k, I^*_k) $ at $k=2$ and $k=6$. The supplementary Figure \ref{fig:stacked_control_map} plots the differences between the respective control maps at those two hours.

