\subsection{Degradation-aware Firming}
BESS cycling adversely affects its useful lifetime. As the battery ages, both its usable energy capacity $\mathrm{SoC}_{\max}$, and its roundtrip efficiency $\eta$ drop. In this section we consider a modified stepwise cost function that mitigates degradation by penalizing deep cycles.  
In the context of dynamic control, degradation cost functions have been used in \citeA{Heyman_degradation,Abdulla2017OptimalOO}. Physically, degradation is driven by cumulative lifetime depth-of-discharge (DoD) which is a path-dependent quantity that requires, e.g., the rainflow counting algorithm and is incompatible with dynamic programming. Consequently, we adapt the approach of \citeA{Heyman_degradation}, modeling degradation as a nonlinear function of the SoC and the charge/discharge rate:
\begin{align}\label{eq:Phi}
\Phi (B,I)  =  \left(1- 0.5 (\frac{I}{I_{\max}})^2 \right)\cdot  \max(-B,0),
\end{align}
leading to the modified stepwise cost function: 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fbl}
    f_{BL}(X_k,B_k,M_k) :=\tilde{f}(X_k,B_k,M_k) + \lambda_{BL} \cdot \Phi (B_k,I_k),
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{BL}$ is the weight of the Battery Lifetime penalty. The second term in \eqref{eq:Phi} increases as $I \downarrow I_{\min}$ or $B \downarrow B_{\min}$ penalizing discharges of nearly empty battery that are associated with high DoD. By considering \eqref{eq:fbl}, the controller prefers more shallow cycling.

To assess the impact of our degradation proxy $\Phi$, we evaluate battery degradation over realized dispatch trajectories after optimizing with $f_{BL}(\cdot)$. Specifically, the resulting SoC profile $(I^*_k)$ is decomposed into charging- and discharging half-cycles using rainflow cycle counting \shortcite{yuanyuan}. The corresponding degradation over the operational day $d$ for asset $\ell$ is then calculated according to:
\begin{equation}
 L^{\ell,d} (\mathbf{c}, \mathbf{d})=\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathbf{c}|} \frac{\Theta\left(c_i\right)}{2}+\sum_{i=1}^{|\mathbf{d}|} \frac{\Theta\left(d_i\right)}{2},
\end{equation}
where $\bold{c}$ (resp.~$\bold{d}$) represents the depth-of-charge (depth-of-discharge) vector for the charging (discharging) half-cycles in $(I_k^*)$ and 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:theta}
\Theta(\text{DoD}) = \left(5.24 \times 10^{-4}\right) (\text{DoD})^{2.03}.
\end{equation}
Above, the constant up front in \eqref{eq:theta} implies that a single daily cycle of full 100\% discharge followed by a full charge would lead to a BESS lifetime of $1/(5.24 \cdot 10^{-4})/365 \simeq 5.2$ years. 

Figure~\ref{fig:degradation_trajectory} explores the performance of the controller under $f_{BL}$ for $\lambda_{BL} \in \{0, 0.1, 0.2\}$ within the case study of Section \ref{sec:5.2}. As expected, larger $\lambda_{BL}$ values result in more muted charging and discharging rates in response to departures from the dispatch target. This pattern is clear in the SoC trajectories in the bottom panel, where controllers with higher $\lambda_{BL}$ show smaller SoC fluctuations. However, this benefit is offset by larger deviations $(O_k-F_k)$, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:degradation_trajectory}.
\begin{figure}[ht]
