    \includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{images/Degradation/degradation_trajectory.png}
    \caption{\emph{Top panel:} A representative trajectory of dispatch deviation without firming $(X_k-F_k)$ and with firming $(O_k-F_k)$ over one day $k=0,\ldots, 23$. \emph{Bottom panel:} Corresponding firmed SoC trajectories $I^{*}_k$. We show three scenarios based on battery lifetime penalty $\lambda_{BL} \in \{0, 0.1, 0.2\}$.}
    \label{fig:degradation_trajectory}
\end{figure}


To illustrate, 
we deploy SHADOw-GP within the framework of $f_{BL}$ across $\lambda_{BL} \in \{0,0.02,0.04,\ldots,0.2\}$; higher $\lambda_{BL}$ places greater emphasis on minimizing $\Phi$. Consequently, as $\lambda_{BL}$ is increased, we anticipate observing a reduction in firming performance and an extension in battery lifespan. In this spirit, we introduce the Expected Battery Life ($\mathrm{EBL}$) metric, measured in years:
\begin{align}
    \mathrm{EBL}^{\ell,d}  & := \EE \left[L^{\ell,d}(\mathbf{c},\mathbf{d})^{-1} \right].
\end{align}
 We re-use \eqref{eq:DR} to define Expected Deviation Reduction (EDR) percentage for operational day $d$: 
 \begin{equation}
 \label{eq:DR}
\mathrm{EDR}^{\ell,d}
 := \EE \left[ \frac{\Dev{X^{\ell,d}} - \Dev{O^{\ell,d}}}{\Dev{X^{\ell,d}}} \right] \times 100 \%.
\end{equation}
The left panel of Figure \ref{fig:degradation_tradeoff}  shows a nearly linear change in $\mathrm{EBL}$ and $\mathrm{EDR}$ as a function of $\lambda_{BL}$; both expectations $\EE[\cdot]$ are based on $10^4$ Monte Carlo simulations for the representative day: Foard City Wind Farm on 2018-04-05. The results indicate that thanks to more shallow cycles, the BESS lifetime more than doubles from approximately 5 years when $\lambda_{BL} = 0$ to roughly 12 years when $\lambda_{BL} = 0.2$. As trade-off, this entails an approximately 25\% reduction in $\mathrm{EDR}$ performance.  
The linear pattern of $\mathrm{EBL}$ as a function of $\lambda_{BL}$ implies that our proxy DoD cost $\Phi$ provides a good approximation for the rainflow-based degradation $L^{\ell,d}(\cdot)$ while avoiding the need to introduce new state variables to keep track of DoD.

\subsection{Curtailment Mitigation}

In this section we consider a controller that simultaneously wishes to minimize the firming performance criterion \eqref{eq:tildeF}, as well as the asymmetric criterion of minimizing Expected Cumulative Violation ($\mathrm{ECV}$) with respect to thresholds $(\overline{M}^{\ell,d}_k)$:
\begin{align}\label{eq:tvc}
\mathrm{ECV}^{\ell,d} = \EE\left[\sum_{k=0}^{23} \text{max}(X^{\ell,d}_k-B^{\ell,d}_k-\overline{M}^{\ell,d}_k,0) \right].
\end{align}
One motivation for the latter objective is curtailment mitigation: avoiding excess generation that might lead to forced output reduction by the grid operator. Curtailment has been a common feature in grids with high share of renewables and is driven by over-generation and limited headroom to regulate down, leading to wasted energy and ultimately additional costs. See e.g.~\citeA{curtailment_GA} for wind turbine curtailment using dynamic programming. 

We interpret the exogenously given $(\overline{M}^{\ell,d}_k)$ as the curtailment threshold, so that \eqref{eq:tvc} proxies the aggregate energy curtailed over the day. 
Alternatively, a constant $\overline{M}^{\ell,d}$ arises in the context of transmission line capacity limits at the point of interconnection (POI) for the hybrid asset. According to \citeA{LBNL}, some hybrid assets are oversized relative to POI limits, so that $\overline{M}^{\ell,d} < 1$ and the BESS must be charging when renewable output is close to its nameplate capacity. 


The controller aims to optimally redirect any generation above $\overline{M}_k$ towards BESS, while continuing to firm towards the dispatch target $M_k \le \overline{M}_k$, leading to the  
Curtailment Mitigation (CM) stepwise cost criterion:
\begin{equation}
 f_{\text{CM}}(X_k,B_k,M_k) := \tilde{f}(X_k,B_k,M_k) + \lambda_{CM} \cdot \text{max}(X_k-B_k-\overline{M}_k,0). 
\end{equation} 
\noindent The right panel of Figure \ref{fig:degradation_tradeoff} demonstrates the trade-off between firming and violation costs, $\mathrm{EDR}$ and $\mathrm{ECV}$ respectively, for a range of weights $\lambda_{CM} \in [0,1]$, setting $\overline{M}_k := 1.05\cdot M_k$ i.e., 5\% above the dispatch target $M_k$. The relationship between ECV and $\lambda_{CM}$ is highly non-linear with a significant change between $\lambda_{CM} = 0$ and $\lambda_{CM} = 0.2$. We observe that taking $\lambda_{CM}=1$ results in a $40\%$ decrease in $\mathrm{ECV}$ compared to $\lambda_{CM} = 0$, trading off for only $6\%$ decrease in $\mathrm{EDR}$ performance, 
suggesting that the cost criterion $f_{CM}$ does a good job at peak shaving. 

