 \\Now we show that $(Q_j^n:f)=S$ for all $j\neq 1$. 
 Consider a primary component $Q_j$ such that $j\neq 1$. Suppose $y_1^{\beta_1}\in Q_j$. Since $u_2,\ldots,u_r\in Q_j$, there exists $x_{p_i}^{\alpha_{p_i}}\in Q_j\setminus Q$ for some $2\leq i\leq t_p$ and $2\leq p\leq r$. Note that $y_{1}^{(n-1)\beta_{1}}x_{p_i}^{\alpha_{p_i}}\in Q_j^n$ and $y_{1}^{(n-1)\beta_{1}}x_{p_i}^{\alpha_{p_i}}$ divides $f$. Therefore $f\in Q_j^n$. Suppose $y_1^{\beta_1}\notin Q_j$. Then there exists $2\leq i\leq t_1$ such that $x_{1_i}^{\alpha_{1_i}}\in Q_j$. Note that $x_{1_i}^{n\alpha_{1_i}}\in Q_j^n$ and $x_{1_i}^{n\alpha_{1_i}}$ divides $f$. Therefore $f\in Q_j^n$. Thus $(I^n:f)=(Q_1^n:f)\bigcap (\bigcap\limits_{m=2}^r (Q_m^n:f))=P$ and 
