A histogram of the distribution of pion vertex's is displayed in the right panel of 
\textcolor{red}{You have to explain your nomenclature. I.e. what is reconstructed vs predicted}
Fig.~\ref{fig:pion_vertex}. The mean absolute difference between the predicted and the truth was $1.1$ cm, compared to the mean absolute difference between the reconstructed and the truth of $3.3 cm$. In addition, a Gaussian fit to the error distributions yielded a standard deviation of $3.3$ for the predicted compared to a standard deviation of $11.6$ for the reconstructed. We were focusing on just getting the pion vertex here, so we had a very low ratio of $\Lambda$s of $1/50$. 
\textcolor{red}{This just seems confusing. I would just discuss the scattering vertex relative to the electron. You did not previous describe the scattering process with enough detail so the below can be understood.}
This result was just for the pion vertex. What we want though is the pion vertex minus the scattered electron vertex. The Scattered electron vertex is taken as the location of the collision. The background pions are all  created here and pions from $\Lambda$ are made later. This pion vertex minus electron vertex is what is useful for improving the signal to background ratio.  The left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:pion_vertex} shows a histogram similar to the right, but using this new pion vertex. Additionally, we also evaluated on a dataset with a high ratio of $\Lambda$ of about $50\%$. 

The blue curve (representing the GNN) in Fig.~\ref{fig:pion_vertex} demonstrates  better performance at predicting pions with a larger vertex's, compared to the green (representing the reconstruction). The long tail of the green that goes into the negative pions is a big reasons for this. Pions with a larger vertex are of particular interest as those are most likely to be $\Lambda$ events. To further show this, the right panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:sb_GNN} compares the absolute error of the GNN and REC predictions for different pion vertex's (the vertex was gotten from Monte-Carlo simulations). Absolute error is taken as $|prediction-truth|$. The GNN has a lower mean error for all pion vertex's, especially for those with high vertices. Additionally, the GNN has has a significantly lower standard deviation than the rec.  

Finally, we generated several scatter plots, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:scatter}, to further illustrate the correlations between the GNN and rec with the truth values. The y-axis of both graphs is the Montre-Carlo pion vertex. For the x-axis, the left is the GNN prediction and the right is the reconstruction prediction. The left follows the truth fairly closely which means that a higher true pion vertex leads to a high GNN prediction pion vertex. Additionally, there is a noticeable cluster in the center, indicating that the GNN assigns uncertain predictions around zero.. There is a clear separation between these though. The right has a much larger grouping around true pion vertex zero and does not have a strong correlation between true vertex and reconstructed vertex.

Finally, we generated several scatter plots, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:scatter}, to further illustrate the correlations between the GNN and rec with the truth values. In the right panel Fig.~\ref{fig:scatter} a roughly linear trend is observed. As the true vertex increases, the  GNN predicts a corresponding increase in the pion vertex. Additionally, there is a noticeable cluster in the center, indicating that the GNN assigns uncertain predictions around zero.  In contrast, the left panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:scatter}, shows a mostly flat correlation. The rec algorithm performs particularly poorly for vertices greater than or equal to zero, distributing them widely. It also exhibits a far greater range of predictions ($-150$ cm to $150$cm) than either the GNN or the truth. Notably, there is no strong upward trend in the data, indicating that as pions are produced later, the rec algorithm does not reconstruct a later vertex accurately. Overall, predictions align more closely with the true values compared to the rec. While not perfect, he GNN performs better, especially for high pion vertices, and is more consistent and tightly clustered. In contrast, the rec does not predict the true values accurately, lacks any discernible trend, and exhibits a much wider spread in both the range of predictions and the reconstructed vertex locations. 

