Therefore, we have.
\begin{theorem}[{\bf \cite[Theorem 3.3]{Luo-Liu16} and \cite[Theorem 4.5]{WZD12}}]\label{theo:q=3/4}
$\chi(3)=\chi(4)=2$.
\end{theorem}

The fractal squares $K(3,\mathcal{D})$ with $\#\mathcal{D}=5$ are  divided into $21$ congruent classes. See \cite{Luo-Liu16} for a complete list.

For five of them, as illustrated in \cite[Fig. A.1]{Luo-Liu16}, $K(3,\mathcal{D})$ is a Cantor set.

For six of them, as illustrated in \cite[Fig. A.2]{Luo-Liu16},  $K(3,\mathcal{D})$ is connected hence is a Peano continuum. Two of those continua are homeomorphic, with nontrivial fundamental group. The other four are dendrites, such as $K(3,\mathcal{D}_i)(i=1,2)$ with 
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:D_T}
\mathcal{D}_1&=&\{(i,0):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(1,1), (1,2)\}\\
\mathcal{D}_2&=&\{(i,1):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(1,0), (1,2)\}
\end{eqnarray} 
It is known that $K(3,\mathcal{D}_1)$ and $K(3,\mathcal{D}_2)$ are not homeomorphic \cite[Theorem 3.10]{Luo-Liu16}. On the other hand, the other two are  homeomorphic with $K(3,\mathcal{D}_2)$. Here, by checking the digit sets one may figure out how to choose the underlying homeomorphisms to be even an affine map.

The rest ten, as illustrated in \cite[Fig. A.3]{Luo-Liu16}, have both point components and non-degenerate ones,  which are parallel line segments. The one with digit set $\mathcal{D}_0$ (as given in Figure \ref{fig:Hata_1}) has been addressed in Example \ref{exmp:Hata_Graph}. Further set
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:D_nondendrite}
\mathcal{D}_3&=&\{(i,0):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(0,2),(1,2)\}\\
\mathcal{D}_4&=&\{(i,0):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(0,2), (2,2)\}\\
\mathcal{D}_5&=&\{(i,i):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(1,0), (0,2)\}\\
\mathcal{D}_6&=&\{(i,0):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(0,2), (2,1)\}\\
\mathcal{D}_7&=&\{(i,0):0\le i\le 2\}\cup\{(1,2), (2,1)\}
\end{eqnarray} 
In Figure \ref{fig:order_3}, we present for $3\le j\le 6$ the first two approximations of  $K(3,\mathcal{D}_j)$.

