Next, note that the unique point in $U$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $v^2_2$ is $(1+\frac{v^1_1+c-1}{\nicefrac{1}{2} + \delta}, v^2_2)$.
Since any $x \in S$ satisfies $v^1_1 \leq x_1$ and $v^2_2 \leq x_2 \leq w^2_2 \leq v^2_2 + c$,
we obtain that all of $S$ is contained in the quadrangle
\[
    \tilde{Q}^1
    := \conv \left( \left\{
        (1,v^2_2+c),
        (v^1_1,v^2_2+c),
        (v^1_1,v^2_2),
        \left( 1+\frac{v^1_1+c-1}{\nicefrac{1}{2} + \delta}, v^2_2 \right)
    \right\} \right).
\]
We can easily compute the area of $\tilde{Q}^1$ to be
\[
    (1-v^1_1) c + \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{v^1_1+c-1}{\nicefrac{1}{2} + \delta} c
    = \frac{c (c + 2 \delta - 2 v^1_1 \delta)}{1 + 2 \delta}.
\]
Since $v^1_1 \geq -\frac{c}{2}$, we therefore obtain
\[
    |\tilde{Q}^1|
    \leq \frac{c (c (1+\delta) + 2\delta))}{1 + 2 \delta}.
\]
Altogether, the claim holds in this case.
Using analogous constructions for the three cases where $w^1_2 > v^2_2 + (\frac{1}{2} + \delta) c$,
$w^2_1 < v^1_1 + (\frac{1}{2} - \delta) c$, or $w^2_1 > v^1_1 + (\frac{1}{2} + \delta) c$,
we may from now on assume that $|w^1_2 - (v^2_2 + \frac{c}{2})| \leq \delta c$ and $|w^2_1 - (v^1_1 + \frac{c}{2})| \leq \delta c$.

For the second step, we use the additional restrictions on $w^1$ and $w^2$ to find further quadrangles that contain all of $S$.
We define two new lines by
\[
