\paragraph{Convergence Under Refinement.}
\label{sec: bending calibration}
We evaluate the accuracy of our bending model using the linear plate bending benchmark~\cite{liang2025corotational}, and compare its convergence behavior to that of linear FEM under mesh refinement. In this experiment, we simulate a flat square plate with all sides fixed, only bending energy enabled, and a uniform load $B=9.81$ applied across the surface.
The maximum deflection of the plate should be $0.048744 B a^4 (1 - \nu^2)/(Eh^3)$, where $a$ is the side length, $\nu$ is the Poisson's ratio, $E$ is the Young's modulus, and $h$ is the thickness of the plate. We set $E=2$ MPa, $\nu=0.03, h=0.01$ m, and simulate with a large timestep size $\Delta t = 0.05$ s until equilibrium is reached.
As shown in~\autoref{fig: bending calibration}, our reduced integration scheme with $1 \times 1$ quadrature achieves accuracy comparable to linear FEM on a mesh of 1.5K regular triangles while providing a $50\times$ speedup. When constrained to the same runtime, our method achieves errors several orders of magnitude lower. Increasing our quadrature density to $2 \times 2$ yields only marginal improvements in accuracy for this example, despite higher computational cost.
In contrast, linear FEM with right-triangle meshes containing alternating diagonal orientations fails to converge to the analytic solution, highlighting its sensitivity to mesh quality, particularly the configuration of edges.

