\subsection{Stress Tests}

\paragraph{Complex Obstacles}  
To evaluate our method’s ability to handle contact with complex geometry, we simulate a square cloth sheet falling onto a static bunny model. The B-spline surface resolution is set to $125 \times 125$, with an underlying $175 \times 175$ linear mesh used for contact handling and rendering. As shown in~\autoref{fig: cloth on bunny}, our method conforms accurately to the underlying geometry while generating fine-scale wrinkles.

\paragraph{Scalability}  
We assess scalability by simulating a $200 \times 200$ high-resolution cloth sheet interacting with a rotating sphere. A square cloth falls onto a static sphere under gravity and reaches a steady configuration before the sphere begins rotating. Due to frictional contact with both the sphere and the ground, the cloth follows the sphere’s motion, producing rich wrinkle detail (\autoref{fig: cloth on rotating sphere}).

\paragraph{Irregular-Shaped Domains}  
Our method also supports irregularly shaped B-spline surfaces. As shown in~\autoref{fig: clashing cloth drop}, four cloth sheets with non-square shapes are initialized with a velocity of $1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and dropped under gravity onto a square cloth with its four corners fixed. The simulation demonstrates the robustness of our method in handling non-uniform rest shapes.

