\subsubsection{Solving a special position matrix}

Given the non-uniqueness of position matrix solutions for an EDM $E_{P}\in \mathbb{EDM}^{n}$, our approach involves two steps:
\begin{itemize}
        \item Find a special solution $P^{\star}\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times n}$.
        \item Using known onboard sensor positions $\boldsymbol{p}_i, i=1,2,3,4$, determine an orthogonal matrix $N$ and a vector $\boldsymbol{t}$ to obtain $P=NP^{\star}+\boldsymbol{t}\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\top}$, where $P$ has its first four columns aligned with $\boldsymbol{p}_i$, $i=1,2,3,4$.
\end{itemize}

Let $P^{0}$ denote the special solution where the first column $\boldsymbol{p}^{0}_{1}=\mathbf{0}$. For this $P^{0}$, we have:
$$[E_{P}]_{(i,1)}=\|\boldsymbol{p}^{0}_{i}-\boldsymbol{p}^{0}_{1}\|^2=\|\boldsymbol{p}^{0}_{i}\|^2,i=1,2,\ldots,n.$$
