Further observing equation \ref{Phi} $\sim$ equation \ref{BC}, we find that we can achieve positive performance fee if $ B >0 $ and $ B^2 +4C \geq 0 $. However, this is generally not the case, since $B$ is typically negative. Accordingly, the conditions $\frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{ R_{p,t}^{*} }}{Q} > \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{ R_{p,t} }}{Q} $ and $ \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{ (R_{p,t}^{*})^2 }}{Q} < \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{ R_{p,t}^2 }}{Q} $ facilitate genetating positive performance fee, which corresponds to a higher return and a lower risk than the benchmark strategy. Rearranging equation \ref{pf2} once more, we have
\begin{align}
-[\Phi - (\frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{p,t}^{*}}}{Q} - \frac{1+\gamma}{\gamma}) ]^2  + D + \frac{ \sum_{v \neq w}{2R_{p,v}^{*}R_{p,w}^{*}} }{Q^2} - \frac{(Q-1)\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{(R_{p,t}^{*})^2 } }{Q^2} = 0
\label{pf3}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
    D = (\frac{1+\gamma}{\gamma})^2 - 2\frac{1+\gamma}{\gamma}\frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{p,t}}}{Q} + \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{p,t}^2}}{Q}
\end{align}
As shown in equation \ref{pf3}, given the performance of benchmark strategy, despite the existence of uncertain element ($\sum_{v \neq w}{2R_{p,v}^{*}R_{p,w}^{*}}$), however, increasing the return ($\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{p,t}^{*}}/Q$) potentially shift the quadratic curve right and improve the resulting performance fee. Similarly, decreasing the risk ($\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{(R_{p,t}^{*})^2}/Q$) potentially shift the quadratic curve upward and also improve the resulting performance fee. Furthermore, since shifting right has a more direct effect on determining performance fee than shifting upward, the return play a more significant role than the risk.

\subsubsection{Other metrics}
We evaluate the performance of different portfolio strategies over the entire out-of-sample period. Since we derive an optimal portfolio across the risk aversion coefficient $\gamma$ from 1 to 10, we average these out-of-sample measures obtained using different values of $\gamma$. We first introduce the average annualized performance fee $\overline{PF}$ as follows:
\begin{align}
     \overline{PF}=\frac{1}{10}\sum_{\gamma=1}^{10}{\kappa\Phi_2^{\gamma}},
\end{align}
where $\kappa$ denotes a multiplier annualizing the performance fee and $\Phi_2^{\gamma}$ denotes the adopted performance fee solution achieved by using a particular $\gamma$.
\par
In addition to the performance fee, we also employ other out-of-sample metrics to analyze the empirical results. Since the out-of-sample length is the same for all strategies, we directly compare the total out-of-sample portfolio return net of transaction costs. Similarly, we take an average across $\gamma$ from 1 to 10 and the average total out-of-sample portfolio return net of transaction costs $\overline{R_p}$ can be written as: 
\begin{align}
\overline{R_p} = \frac{1}{10}\sum_{\gamma=1}^{10}
\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{p,t}^{\gamma}}, 
\end{align}
where $R_{p,t}^{\gamma}$ denotes the portfolio return net of transaction costs achieved by using a particular $\gamma$ at time t. 
\par
We also care about the risk profile of portfolio and compute the average total out-of-sample portfolio risk $\overline{R_p^2}$ as follows:
\begin{align}
\overline{R_p^2} = \frac{1}{10}\sum_{\gamma=1}^{10}
\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{(R_{p,t}^{\gamma})^2}, 
\end{align}
To enhance empirical analysis, we further decompose the portfolio return net of transaction costs $\overline{R_p}$ into two components: the average total out-of-sample gross portfolio return $\overline{R_g}$ and the average total out-of-sample transaction costs $\overline{TC}$, and which can be written as:
\begin{align}
\overline{R_g} = \frac{1}{10}\sum_{\gamma=1}^{10}
\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{R_{g,t}^{\gamma}},\\
\overline{TC} = \frac{1}{10}\sum_{\gamma=1}^{10}\sum_{t=1}^{Q}{TC_t^{\gamma}},
\end{align}
where $R_{g,t}^{\gamma}$ and $TC_t^{\gamma}$ denote the gross portfolio return and the transaction cost achieved by using a particular $\gamma$ at time t respectively.
\par
The forecasting accuracy is evaluated by the aggregate RMSE measure as defined above, but replace the validation set with the test set. Furthermore, we examine the variation characteristic of different conditional moment estimators. The $L_2$-norm and the Frobenius norm are employed to measure the variation levels ($VL$) of different return forecasting series and estimated covariance matrices respectively, which can be represented as:
\begin{align}
VL_{mean} = \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q-1}{|| \hat{r}_{t+1}-\hat{r}_{t} ||_2}}{Q-1} = \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q-1}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^{N}{(\hat{r}_{i,t+1}-\hat{r}_{i,t})^2}}}}{Q-1}, \\
VL_{cov} = \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q-1}{|| \hat{\Sigma}_{t+1}-\hat{\Sigma}_{t} ||_F}}{Q-1} = \frac{\sum_{t=1}^{Q-1}{\sqrt{\sum_{i,j}{(\hat{\Sigma}_{ij,t+1}-\hat{\Sigma}_{ij,t})^2}}}}{Q-1}.
\end{align}

